{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Whose voice of living Elohim you heard speaking from the midst of the fire</b>. Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 5:23) said that the reason for <b>living</b> is to distinguish between Elohim and the heavenly host, for He never slumbers of Himself. And it is possible to explain that he mentioned this to say that one who hears the voice of Elohim, with whom is the source of life, his soul cleaves to its root and no longer lives a life of flesh, and Joshua said (Joshua 3:10–11): 'By this you shall know that a living El is in your midst — behold, the ark of the covenant, the Adon of all the earth' — if so, then 'and He swore by the Life of the worlds' (Daniel 12:7) is an oath by His great Name."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The word יתנועע ('slumbers/dozes') is rendered literally; the argument contrasts Elohim's wakefulness with created beings who tire."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:13:50.583139909Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}